+Computes second-order gradients for voxel grid features to support Eikonal regularization, solving the memory overhead of naive automatic differentiation. It differs from Mean Feature Aggregation by Voxel by implementing backward passes for gradient computation rather than first-order mean aggregation.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the 3D sparse UNet forward pass. Voxelization is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of points. Hashing and unique operations are $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$ with hash tables.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of occupied voxels and the feature dimension $n$. Sparse data structures minimize memory usage for empty regions.
-*   **Strengths:** Produces dense, globally consistent geometric features from sparse LiDAR data. The 3D sparse UNet captures local and global context [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality and density of the input LiDAR data. Aggregation across frames assumes static scenes or requires precise synchronization [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When LiDAR data is available and geometric context is critical for rendering, especially in large-scale outdoor scenes like street environments [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When only RGB images are available, or when real-time performance is critical and the overhead of the sparse UNet is prohibitive.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the voxelization step ($O(N)$ for $N$ points) and the 3D sparse UNet encoding. The UNet complexity depends on the number of occupied voxels and the network depth [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of unique occupied voxels and the feature dimension $n$ [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Leverages the strength of both LiDAR and visual data by providing a dense geometric context. The 3D sparse UNet allows learning from a global context of scene geometry [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires aggregation of multiple frames to construct a dense point cloud, which may be computationally expensive for large sequences [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When high-quality and complete scene rendering is required, particularly in street scenes where LiDAR data is available [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from the LidaRF paper [S1], this method was introduced to leverage the strength of LiDAR data for neural radiance fields in street scenes. It builds on the success of 3D sparse UNets in 3D perception frameworks [10, 33, 22, 47] to encode scene geometry. The approach was developed to address the sparsity and noise of single-frame LiDAR scans by aggregating data across sequences and encoding it into a dense neural representation.
+Originating from the LidaRF paper, this method was introduced to delve into LiDAR for Neural Radiance Fields on street scenes [S1]. It builds on the success of 3D perception frameworks that use sparse UNets for geometry encoding [S1]. The method is part of a pipeline that jointly learns LiDAR and visual features for high-quality rendering [S1].
 
